WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.037 --> 00:00:02.100
- "Dungeons &amp; Dragons"
has altered the deal.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.530
Pray they do not alter it any further

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:06.180
because for years Wizards of the Coast,

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.770
which publishes "Dungeons &amp; Dragons"

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.830
has operated under an open
gaming license, an OGL

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:11.663
that allowed people

00:00:11.663 --> 00:00:13.650
to create and profit
from unofficial content

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.720
in the "Dungeons &amp; Dragons" universe.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:16.800
Or does it?

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.750
It's time to think like a rules lawyer.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:19.790
The idea is that the OGL

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:21.660
is a legal framework that allows people

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.960
to use the rules and ideas
of the role-playing games

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.520
to create their own works.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.220
And it was originally
released in the year 2000

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.420
by Wizards of the Coast.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:31.440
And a lot of people

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.860
have come to rely on the terms of the OGL

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.440
to make and publish their own content

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.660
under the aegis of Wizards of the Coast.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.970
But that was version 1 of the OGL,

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:45.240
now, according to a leaked
version 1.1 of the OGL,

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.460
which has not officially been adopted,

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:48.990
makes huge sweeping changes

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.030
that will affect anyone that makes content

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.070
even related to "Dungeons &amp; Dragons."

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.620
But this leaked version
1.1 update to the OGL

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:56.640
is a mixed bag.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:57.780
It purports to crack down

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.060
on sexist, homophobic
and transphobic content

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.790
that is created with the
"Dungeons &amp; Dragons" name.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.340
It purports to grant a perpetual license

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:06.660
to Wizards of the Coast

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.330
for anyone that makes
content using that license.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.130
It purports to require certain people

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.320
who make content using that license,

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.900
to report that usage
to Wizards of the Coast

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.410
and if you make over $750,000 in revenue,

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:22.350
you have to pay certain
royalties up to 25%

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.890
to Wizards of the Coast on
certain marginal income.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:27.750
These are big changes.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.340
Now it's important to distinguish the OGL

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.710
from the actual "Dungeon &amp; Dragons" rules.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:34.320
The "Dungeons &amp; Dragons"
operates under a set of rules

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.990
that's up to version 5e at this point,

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.750
and the OGL is not the
"Dungeons &amp; Dragons" rules.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.460
And generally speaking,
those rules are found

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.620
in the Systems Reference
Document, the SRD.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.240
That's a 400 page document

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.400
containing just about
everything you need to know

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.110
about playing "D&amp;D".

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:50.820
The main benefit of the OGL,

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.880
whether you're talking about
version 1 or version 1.1,

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.180
is that it allows people to
use and reproduce the SRD,

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:58.013
the rules.

00:01:58.013 --> 00:01:59.280
But for people who make content

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.960
that just simply works with the rules

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.690
but doesn't reproduce them,
well there's a big question

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.000
about whether you need the OGL at all.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.380
Now, we'll come back to that later.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.690
Now, these sweeping changes
caused a huge uproar

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.820
among fans and content creators alike.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:13.170
But like I said, it's a mixed bag.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.390
On the plus side, it provides
a relatively clear path

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:16.620
to allow almost anyone to create

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.470
officially licensed content,
and that's pretty rare.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:20.617
It's not like Disney says,

00:02:20.617 --> 00:02:22.950
"Sure, you can make any
Mickey Mouse content you want.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:26.460
Just pay us if you happen to
make over $750,000 in revenue."

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:27.720
And if these changes were coming

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.910
from a position of absolute restriction,

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.520
like Disney has a lockdown on their IP,

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.020
then people would probably rejoice

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.700
that they have this pathway

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.360
to make officially licensed
content and profit from it.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.700
But as it stands, we're
coming from the opposite.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:42.720
We're coming from a world

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.610
where almost everything was totally open

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:47.490
and these restrictions are
causing a lot of consternation,

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:50.580
so many people are upset about
this leaked modified OGL.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.050
And the leaked OGL differentiates

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.330
between commercial and
non-commercial content.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:57.900
People making non-commercial
content are fairly unburdened.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.180
And the leak says that
making money via Patreon

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.520
doesn't necessarily count as commercial,

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.470
so that probably covers
a large swath of people

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:06.600
who might otherwise be
upset about these changes.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.100
Now, I'm not gonna dive too deep

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.530
into the changes of this OGL.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:12.270
It's reasonably easy to understand

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.130
to the credit of the
lawyers who drafted it,

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:16.290
and a lot of the coverage
has already described

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:19.200
what the implication of
these purported changes are.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.180
And there are some issues
as far as enforceability,

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.193
and promissory estoppel, and reliance.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:28.470
Can you revoke a license that
was granted in perpetuity?

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:29.760
I'm not gonna get into those.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:30.990
For a more detailed breakdown

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:32.790
of the contractual provisions themselves

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:34.590
I'd highly recommend
you check out the nerds

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:36.060
over at "Opening Arguments."

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:37.500
They really go into the details

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.710
of what the original
and the leaked OGL say,

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:41.970
and what they don't say.

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:43.440
It's absolutely the best breakdown

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:44.940
of the contracts themselves.

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:45.773
But I also think

00:03:45.773 --> 00:03:48.480
almost everyone is
missing the biggest issue.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:49.800
And what I want to cover today

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.290
is one of the more fundamental
flaws with this whole setup,

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:55.110
and I think, frankly,
it changes everything.

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:56.610
And as it happens, this whole controversy

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:58.020
is a perfect opportunity to teach people

00:03:58.020 --> 00:03:59.790
about the difference between
copyright and trademarks,

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:00.960
so you know, (laughs) win-win.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.820
But the original OGL has
been around for a while

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.820
and it was intended to provide
an open platform for players,

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:07.800
and for some of the critical
background on that history

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.950
I turn to the "D&amp;D" expert
and ubernerd, Matt Colville.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.290
- The products that they
make for "Dungeons &amp; Dragons"

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:16.170
tend to sell best to players.

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:18.090
Well, adventures don't make a lot of money

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.940
because only one of the four
or five players at the table

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:22.800
buy them, but you still gotta make 'em.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.930
So his argument was, "Why
don't we open these rules

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:26.310
and make it so that people,

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:28.380
anybody can make products for us?

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:30.750
And that way third party
companies will come along

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:32.850
and they will make adventures for us,

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:34.500
and they'll make other stuff
too, but we don't care,

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:36.240
and that way they'll support our product."

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:40.537
And there was also some
verbiage in there about how,

00:04:40.537 --> 00:04:43.380
"And if those companies improve our rules

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:44.790
and it's published under the OGL,

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:46.860
then we can take those
improvements, right?

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:48.390
And then our game will improve."

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:49.223
And that was a, you know,

00:04:49.223 --> 00:04:51.240
a very egalitarian way
of looking at things.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:52.073
And then fast forward

00:04:52.073 --> 00:04:54.060
and Wizards of the Coast
releases a 4th edition

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:57.810
which was very controversial
and alienated a lot of fans.

00:04:57.810 --> 00:04:59.550
And so they sort of freaked out

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:00.870
and released a 5th edition.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:03.150
4th edition wasn't released
under the Open Gaming License,

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:06.300
it was released under a
different license called the GSL

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:09.180
which was much more
draconic and restrictive.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:13.200
And then 5th edition was
released under the old OGL,

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.640
which was much more open and broad,

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:16.890
and empowers people to
make the stuff they want.

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:18.510
Privately, the reason Ryan said

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:22.020
he wanted to talk the
board into opening "D&amp;D"

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:23.520
was because he did not like the idea

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.730
that any one company
would control this hobby.

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:27.930
Like he was like, "I don't think,

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:29.250
I think "D&amp;D" should belong to everybody

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:31.350
and this is the best
thing way I can think of

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:32.377
to make that happen."

00:05:32.377 --> 00:05:34.950
"D&amp;D" is, I think, more of
a folk tradition and a hobby

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:36.480
than it is a classical brand.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:39.120
And it has always relied
on the people who play it,

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:40.680
more so than any other game I can think of

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:42.960
except, you know, perhaps
like "Skyrim," right?

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:44.610
Where there's so many mods for it.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:47.280
It's always relied on the
audience to create content for it.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.380
Things like the Thief

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:52.380
come from people playing
in Los Angeles and coming,

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:55.410
a new class of literally calling
up Gary Gygax on the phone

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:57.300
and saying, "Hey, we got this cool idea."

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:58.470
And he was like, "Oh, that's cool.

00:05:58.470 --> 00:05:59.580
I'd probably do it like this."

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:01.800
And all of a sudden there's
a thief in the game, right?

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:02.640
So that relationship

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.470
between the players and
the people making the game

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:09.000
has always been a lot more
gray and weirdly nuanced

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:10.950
than almost any other game I can think of.

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:12.300
- Okay, so with that
background out of the way,

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:13.200
let's talk about the difference

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:14.910
between copyright and trademark.

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:16.740
I promise it will be worth it.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:20.670
Now, copyright is a certain
legal protection for expression.

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:22.530
When you have an original
work of authorship,

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:24.810
whether it is a movie or a novel

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:27.150
and you affix it to a tangible medium,

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.700
well then copyright will
protect that expression.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:31.830
Copyright does not protect ideas,

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:34.800
it does not protect processes,
it protects expression,

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:37.230
whether that expression
is in the form of words,

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.390
or a painting, or a video,

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:41.940
copyright will provide
quite a lot of protection.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:44.430
Trademark is about serving to identify

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:47.340
the source of a particular
product or service

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:48.750
to distinguish that product

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:51.990
from other sellers in the marketplace.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:52.980
The idea with trademark

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:54.750
is you don't want consumers confused.

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:57.000
You want people to actually
know what they're buying

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.470
at the time of sale.

00:06:58.470 --> 00:06:59.850
And trademark is a little bit different

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:01.890
and it can last forever.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.980
As long as someone is actually
selling the trademark good,

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:06.390
that trademark will continue

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:08.460
for the protection of consumers.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:12.150
Now, "Dungeons &amp; Dragons"
as a tabletop RPG product

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:13.080
involves both.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:16.530
It contains copyrightable
pieces of expression

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:19.290
as well as trademarkable elements

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:22.530
that are meant to identify
it in the marketplace.

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:25.267
Let's use a board game that
everyone is familiar with,

00:07:25.267 --> 00:07:27.450
"Monopoly" as an example here.

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:30.570
The name "Monopoly" is trademarked.

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:33.600
There is a long history of
the "Monopoly" trademark

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:36.660
that dealt with years
and years of litigation.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:38.160
Let's put it aside for the moment.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:40.650
The logo of "Monopoly"
is also trademarked.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:42.780
The official "Monopoly" board,

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:46.020
the actual squares and everything
on it, that's copyrighted,

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:48.780
that is expression, it's
as if it were a painting.

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:50.220
And then sometimes within board games

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:52.140
you have specifically detailed characters

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:53.280
that can be copyrighted.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:54.960
For example, in "Monopoly"

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.750
rich Uncle Pennybags is
a copyrighted character.

00:07:57.750 --> 00:07:59.610
And in "Clue" you have Colonel Mustard,

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:01.380
those are copyrighted characters.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.890
- I'm a real guest.
(fanfare trumpet music)

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:07.470
- But the name of the game
"Monopoly" is trademarked

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:08.580
so you can't go out there

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:11.910
and create a game called
"Monopoly" and sell it,

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:15.030
even if the rules of the game
are completely different.

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:16.440
But what about the opposite?

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:19.470
What if you created a game
that had a different name

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:22.380
but followed the same
rules as another game?

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:25.320
Well, that takes us to
the fundamental flaw

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:27.720
of this whole entire controversy,

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:31.470
which is that you can't
copyright the rules to a game.

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:33.450
You can't copyright a process.

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:34.283
Now this goes back

00:08:34.283 --> 00:08:37.200
to an extremely old
copyright Supreme Court case

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:40.200
in the late 19th century
called Baker versus Selden

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.330
where someone tried to copyright

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:44.430
a particular method of bookkeeping.

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:47.640
In that case, a guy created
a method of bookkeeping,

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:50.838
i.e accounting and
published a book about it.

00:08:50.838 --> 00:08:53.430
The book did okay, but it really took off

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:55.290
when another person created another book

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:58.410
of basically just forms that
used the same methodology

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:00.690
but didn't credit the original person.

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:03.420
The original author sued,
but the Supreme Court said

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:05.760
that there was no copyright available

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:08.100
because you can't copyright a method.

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:09.810
And that idea was later codified

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:12.397
in the Copyright Act of
1976, which said, quote,

00:09:12.397 --> 00:09:14.010
"In no case does copyright protection

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:15.510
for an original work of authorship

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:18.060
extend to any idea
procedure, process, system,

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:21.090
method of operation, concept,
principle, or discovery,

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:23.010
regardless of the form
in which it is described,

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:26.100
explained, illustrated,
or embodied in such work."

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:26.933
So, for example,

00:09:26.933 --> 00:09:30.127
if you wondered why
everyone could play the game

00:09:30.127 --> 00:09:31.950
"Words with Friends" on Facebook,

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:34.980
even though it was an
exact clone of "Scrabble",

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:36.157
well that's the reason.

00:09:36.157 --> 00:09:38.700
"Scrabble" can't have copyright protection

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:40.680
over the rules of the game,

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:42.810
it can only really get
trademark protection

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:46.110
over the name "Scrabble" not
the rules contained therein.

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:47.610
Now, there is a little
bit of a wrinkle here

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:50.670
in the sense that if you
had a manual of rules,

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:52.530
you do get a thin copyright

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:55.500
over the exact expression over the rules

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:57.570
as laid out in that rule book.

00:09:57.570 --> 00:09:59.040
However, you don't get a copyright

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:01.710
over the rules and processes themselves.

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:04.140
So in a sense, you can have something

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:07.590
that has exactly the same
mechanics, the same processes,

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:11.040
it just can't necessarily
have the exact same expression

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:13.530
if you were to reproduce
the rules perfectly,

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:15.450
but that's kind of a minor quibble.

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:16.283
So how does this relate

00:10:16.283 --> 00:10:18.960
to the Wizards of the
Coast update to the OGL?

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:21.060
Well, I think, and I could be wrong,

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:22.950
that Wizards of the Coast is assuming

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:25.500
that anytime anyone creates any content

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:29.250
that happens to use the
rules of "Dungeons &amp; Dragons"

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:31.560
that it's covered by the OGL.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:32.550
I don't think that's the case.

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:35.640
Now, this is not legal
advice. Your mileage may vary.

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:38.970
But as we discussed you
can't copyright the rules,

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:42.330
you can't copyright the
methodology and processes

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:44.910
and that takes us to the thing
that no one is talking about.

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:46.740
Here's part of the secret,

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:50.040
the original OGL kind of
sucks, and here's why.

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:50.873
The main benefit of the OGL

00:10:50.873 --> 00:10:53.520
was that it allowed
people to include the SRD,

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:56.010
the System Reference
Document in their material.

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:57.660
If your material doesn't require

00:10:57.660 --> 00:11:01.590
a reprinting or quoting of
the SRD or the 5e rules,

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:04.050
then you probably don't need the OGL.

00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:06.570
The original OGL allowed
people to use what it called,

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:08.070
quote, "Open Game Content"

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:11.130
meaning, quote, "The game
mechanic and includes the methods,

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:13.170
procedures, processes, and routines

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:15.420
to the extent such content does not embody

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:16.650
the product identity."

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:18.510
Now, it did not allow people to use,

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:20.437
quote, "Product Identity" meaning, quote,

00:11:20.437 --> 00:11:22.320
"Product and product line names, logos,

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:24.930
identifying marks including
trade dress; artifacts;

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:27.390
creatures characteristics;
stories, storylines, plots,

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:30.570
thematic elements, dialogue,
incidents, language, artwork,

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:33.090
symbols, designs," et
cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:35.490
So based on what we've
already discussed here,

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:36.930
I'm not sure that there was much benefit

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:38.400
to the original OGL.

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:39.233
It mainly allowed you

00:11:39.233 --> 00:11:41.460
to use stuff that you were
already allowed to use,

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:43.920
unless you wanted to
actually quote the expression

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:46.170
that was found in the SRD itself.

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:48.510
And the new OGL does
basically the same thing.

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:50.280
The leaked OGL allows licensed people

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:52.890
to use, quote, "Licensed content."

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:55.770
It says, "Usable D&amp;D content",
quote, "'Licensed content.'

00:11:55.770 --> 00:11:57.240
This is Dungeons &amp; Dragons content

00:11:57.240 --> 00:11:59.820
that is included in the SRD version 5.1,

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:01.440
including basic game mechanics,

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:03.900
a curated selection of
classes, monsters spells,

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:06.000
and items that allow you to make content

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:08.820
compatible with Dungeons
&amp; Dragons 5th edition."

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:11.827
And then the new OGL goes on to say,

00:12:11.827 --> 00:12:15.180
"Not usable D&amp;D content"
quote, "'Unlicensed content.'

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:16.830
This is Dungeons &amp; Dragons content

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:19.050
that has been or later will
be produced as official,

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:21.030
that is released by Wizards of the Coast

00:12:21.030 --> 00:12:23.220
or any of its predecessors or successors,

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:26.430
and is not present in the SRD version 5.1.

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:27.930
Unlicensed content includes things

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:29.490
like the most famous Dungeons &amp; Dragons

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:31.080
monsters, characters, magic spells,

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:32.790
and things related to the various settings

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:35.520
used in Dungeons &amp; Dragons
official content over the years,

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:38.077
what the old open license
content referred to as", quote,

00:12:38.077 --> 00:12:39.210
"Product identity."

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:41.010
So both OGLs purported to let you use

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:43.440
game mechanics of "Dungeons &amp; Dragons".

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:44.430
And in my view,

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:47.400
that's stuff that everyone
was always allowed to use.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:49.050
But of course the benefit of the OGLs,

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:51.390
whether it's version 1 or version 1.1

00:12:51.390 --> 00:12:54.060
is that it provides
certainty, so there's that.

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:54.893
But let's differentiate

00:12:54.893 --> 00:12:56.430
between two different types of content.

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:58.110
In one bucket you'll have what I'll call

00:12:58.110 --> 00:12:59.670
reproduction content.

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:01.860
And in the other bucket
you have what I would call

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:03.360
homebrew content.

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:05.460
In this first reproduction bucket

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:07.140
you have things that would
probably be considered,

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:09.150
from a copyright perspective,
derivative works.

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:11.880
That is works that are based
on a copyrighted property

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:13.470
that Wizards of the Coast owns.

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:15.210
So for example, if you made a T-shirt

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:16.740
that features a character that is based on

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:17.947
an official Wizards of the Coast

00:13:17.947 --> 00:13:19.470
"Dungeons &amp; Dragons" campaign,

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:21.630
and you put that on a T-shirt,
even if you paint it yourself

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:23.400
well that's probably a derivative work

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:25.500
and you need to get the permission
of Wizards of the Coast.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:26.333
And to their credit,

00:13:26.333 --> 00:13:28.680
Wizards of the Coast has a fan art policy

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:30.870
that allows fans to create fan art.

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:31.703
And this is on their website,

00:13:31.703 --> 00:13:33.150
and I don't think it's changed.

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:36.120
But also in this bucket is
stuff that reproduces the SRD.

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:38.760
Remember I said that
they do have a copyright

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:42.540
over the actual exact
wording that's in the SRD,

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:45.840
and recall that both the
original OGL and version 1.1

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:48.060
allow people to reproduce the SRD.

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:49.800
So if your livelihood

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:54.630
relies on actually reproducing
the expression of the SRD,

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:57.360
you would need to abide
by the terms of the OGL,

00:13:57.360 --> 00:13:58.830
and I don't think that has changed.

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:02.040
And maybe you want to quote
large sections of the SRD

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:03.450
to put it into a reference manual,

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:06.990
or maybe you want to put
the entire SRD into a PDF

00:14:06.990 --> 00:14:08.490
along with your original content,

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:12.030
say if you created an
entirely new set of rules,

00:14:12.030 --> 00:14:14.760
but it starts with a basis of the SRD

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:16.050
and you don't want to have people

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:18.240
referencing back to the old SRD.

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:19.770
Well that is probably covered.

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:22.860
And to their credit,
both versions of the OGL

00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:24.240
allows people to do that,

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:27.810
if you want to quote the SRD
it creates a pathway for that.

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:30.090
But this reproductive
bucket consists of things

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:32.160
that are based on things
that Wizards of the Coast

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:33.420
can actually copyright.

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:35.280
In the second bucket,
and this is the thing

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:37.680
that I think most people are upset about,

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:41.080
would be homebrewed
adventures and campaigns

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:44.550
whose only connection
to Wizards of the Coast

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:46.470
is the fact that they use the rules

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:48.960
in "Dungeons &amp; Dragons 5e."

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:50.760
In this bucket I'm kind of
thinking about the things

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:53.370
that you see see on "Critical
Role" or "Dimension 20"

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:56.250
where they create a campaign
that is 100% original.

00:14:56.250 --> 00:14:58.050
The characters are completely new,

00:14:58.050 --> 00:15:00.300
the settings are completely new

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:04.020
and the only connection
that these campaigns have

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:05.910
to "Dungeons &amp; Dragons"
and Wizards of the Coast

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:08.400
is that they rely on the
rules that are set out

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:10.860
in "Dungeons &amp; Dragons 5e."

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:13.740
As we discussed, I don't
think Wizards of the Coast

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:16.890
has much protection over
the rules themselves,

00:15:16.890 --> 00:15:19.110
the processes and methodologies

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:21.630
contained in the "Dungeons
&amp; Dragons" rules.

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:25.020
So if you create an
otherwise original campaign

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:26.880
where the only connection
is the fact that it uses

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:29.730
the rules that are set out in "D&amp;D 5e",

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:31.530
I don't think that runs afoul of the OGL.

00:15:31.530 --> 00:15:33.300
In fact, I don't think you need permission

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:35.700
from Wizards of the Coast in any case.

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:39.540
Even if you videotape and
livestream that campaign,

00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:41.550
I don't think you need their permission

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:43.350
because at the end of the day,

00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:46.200
Wizards of the Coast doesn't really have

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:49.260
any kind of IP protection
over the rules themselves.

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:51.060
Now admittedly, there's
a lot of gray area here

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:53.610
because it's not as simple
as one bucket for fan art

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:55.650
and one bucket for homebrew.

00:15:55.650 --> 00:15:57.390
Wizards of the Coast and "D&amp;D"

00:15:57.390 --> 00:15:59.400
has all kinds of different characters.

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:00.720
Some are pre-made.

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:03.030
Now arguably, those characters

00:16:03.030 --> 00:16:05.340
are often in the service of
the mechanics of the game.

00:16:05.340 --> 00:16:09.060
They define the strength
or speed or dexterity

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:10.350
of these particular characters,

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:12.240
but there's also a backstory there.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:15.600
So does that create the kind
of character that is protected,

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:17.970
like Colonel Mustard or James Bond,

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:20.610
or is that simply a rule in the game

00:16:20.610 --> 00:16:23.640
that functions within
that rule-based universe?

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:24.690
That's a tough question.

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:27.420
But I'm also skeptical
that the changes to the OGL

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:30.480
affect virtual tabletops
or Kickstarters, as well.

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:31.920
Again, the virtual tabletops

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:35.820
are basically using the mechanics
of the rules themselves,

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:37.200
which I don't think Wizards of the Coast

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:38.310
has much protection over.

00:16:38.310 --> 00:16:39.780
And same with Kickstarters

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:42.870
over original campaigns and adventures.

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:44.130
You know, again, if you're creating

00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:46.830
an entirely original world
that just simply happens to use

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:49.020
the rules of "Dungeons &amp; Dragons",

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:51.690
I'm not sure that there's
much that Wizards of the Coast

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:52.950
can do about that.

00:16:52.950 --> 00:16:53.850
Though, at the same time,

00:16:53.850 --> 00:16:55.680
if you are trying to do a Kickstarter

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:57.570
over something that Wizards of the Coast

00:16:57.570 --> 00:16:59.280
actually has protection over,

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:01.110
if you wanted to crowdsource a movie

00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:06.110
based on the mythos and
lore of "Dungeons &amp; Dragons"

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:09.060
that probably is going
to be covered by the OGL.

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:09.930
Though another gray area

00:17:09.930 --> 00:17:11.760
where I'm not sure where
a court would come down

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:14.520
would be, say,
live-streaming a play-through

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:15.967
of an official Wizards of the Coast

00:17:15.967 --> 00:17:17.610
"Dungeons &amp; Dragons" campaign.

00:17:17.610 --> 00:17:19.020
Obviously Wizards of the Coast publishes

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:21.990
a number of official "Dungeons
&amp; Dragons" campaigns,

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:24.750
and if you live-streamed
you and your friends

00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:26.070
playing through that,

00:17:26.070 --> 00:17:28.770
that is using official
"Dungeons &amp; Dragons" content

00:17:28.770 --> 00:17:31.140
over which they do have the copyright.

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:32.040
That's a tough call.

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:33.240
Though I would say that's analogous

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:35.400
to live-streaming a
play-through of a video game,

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:36.990
which is also kind of a gray area.

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:38.790
A video game is copyrighted.

00:17:38.790 --> 00:17:41.550
And it's an open question
whether your playing-through it

00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:45.510
is adding enough original content
to be covered by fair use.

00:17:45.510 --> 00:17:47.670
And the same would go for playing-through

00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:49.980
a "Dungeons &amp; Dragons" official campaign.

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:51.600
Though I do think that
is a separate question

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:53.730
from the, kind of, fully original

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:56.520
homebrew type of situation that
we were just talking about.

00:17:56.520 --> 00:17:58.590
Now, if you are a big player in the space,

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:00.750
like "Critical Role" or "Dimension 20"

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:02.550
there might be some true advantages

00:18:02.550 --> 00:18:03.780
to making sure that everything

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:06.720
is officially licensed
by Wizards of the Coast,

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:08.880
and they might not be able to avoid it.

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:12.360
And certainly if you are
going to create your homebrews

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:13.530
that are not officially licensed,

00:18:13.530 --> 00:18:15.300
you can't say that they
are officially licensed

00:18:15.300 --> 00:18:18.720
and you probably can't use
the name "Dungeons &amp; Dragons"

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:21.600
except in a nominative or
descriptive fair use way

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:23.857
in the sense that you might
be able to say something like,

00:18:23.857 --> 00:18:27.930
"This campaign is compatible
with 'Dungeons &amp; Dragons.'"

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:30.450
Because even with trademark restrictions,

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:34.170
you can describe a thing
by its official name

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:36.570
but you're likely to be
able to compare stuff

00:18:36.570 --> 00:18:39.120
and say that this is compatible

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:41.610
with the rules of "Dungeons &amp; Dragons",

00:18:41.610 --> 00:18:42.600
you're just not allowed to say

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:44.880
that this is from "Dungeons &amp; Dragons"

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:46.530
or somehow officially licensed.

00:18:46.530 --> 00:18:47.760
Anyway, that's just a theory.

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:50.610
A game, legal theory.

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:54.600
And of course the
situation has now changed.

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:56.430
Wizards of the Coast released a statement

00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:59.430
on dndbeyond.com on January 13th.

00:18:59.430 --> 00:19:02.100
In a tongue-in-cheek way, they
said that they were cognizant

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:05.310
of all of the pushback
and, quote, "Rolled a one."

00:19:05.310 --> 00:19:06.157
They say that, quote,

00:19:06.157 --> 00:19:08.070
"The next OGL will contain the provisions

00:19:08.070 --> 00:19:09.720
that allow us to protect and cultivate

00:19:09.720 --> 00:19:12.030
the inclusive environment
we're trying to build

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:16.470
and specify that it covers
only content for TTRPGs.

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:18.150
That means that other expressions

00:19:18.150 --> 00:19:21.450
such as educational and charitable
campaigns, live-streams,

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:23.940
cosplay, VTT-uses, et cetera,

00:19:23.940 --> 00:19:27.060
will remain unaffected by any OGL update.

00:19:27.060 --> 00:19:30.330
Content already released
under version 1.0a

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:32.190
will also remain unaffected.

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:34.830
What it will not contain
is any royalty structure.

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:37.110
It also will not include
the license back provision

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:38.700
that some people were afraid

00:19:38.700 --> 00:19:41.100
was a means for us to steal work.

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:42.810
That thought never crossed our minds.

00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:44.160
Under any new OGL

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:46.260
you will own the content you create.

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:47.093
We won't.

00:19:47.093 --> 00:19:49.380
Any language we put down
will be crystal clear

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:51.270
and unequivocal on that point."

00:19:51.270 --> 00:19:52.710
Now, not only have I been talking

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:55.470
to tons of "D&amp;D" players and GM's,

00:19:55.470 --> 00:19:58.350
I've also been talking to
Wizards of the Coast's lawyers.

00:19:58.350 --> 00:20:01.140
Now there's tons of stuff
going on behind the scenes,

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:02.820
but all the lawyers will officially say

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:05.610
is that their statement is sincere.

00:20:05.610 --> 00:20:08.310
Typical lawyers. Just the worst.

00:20:08.310 --> 00:20:10.710
But look, it's great that they
listened to the criticism.

00:20:10.710 --> 00:20:13.350
I'm sure that the changes will mollify

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:15.780
a lot of what people are
currently upset about.

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:16.980
But I think the point remains,

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:20.460
if you're only making content
that is "D&amp;D" compatible

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:22.710
and doesn't reproduce their IP,

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:25.650
well maybe you never needed
to comply with the OGL

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:26.850
in the first place.

00:20:26.850 --> 00:20:28.080
Maybe the real OGL

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:30.477
was the friends that
we made along the way.

00:20:30.477 --> 00:20:31.310
(bright music)

00:20:31.310 --> 00:20:33.180
Smoke bomb.
(explosion booming)

00:20:33.180 --> 00:20:35.610
Now, normally I would not
cover a topic like this.

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:37.650
This is way too nerdy even for me

00:20:37.650 --> 00:20:39.480
and it's way too in the weeds.

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:41.040
Normally a subject like this

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:44.460
would be covered by a fellow
YouTube attorney, Richard Hoeg.

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:46.560
A few days after Christmas,
he suffered a stroke,

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:47.850
was rushed to the hospital,

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:50.640
and underwent extensive brain surgery.

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:53.280
It seems like he's
through the worst of it,

00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:55.260
but regardless, he's going to have

00:20:55.260 --> 00:20:57.330
an extensive recovery period.

00:20:57.330 --> 00:20:59.520
And Richard has always
been a straight shooter,

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:01.170
and his YouTube channel, Hoeg Law,

00:21:01.170 --> 00:21:04.620
has always been a resource for
insightful legal commentary

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:07.500
on a lot of legal minutiae.

00:21:07.500 --> 00:21:09.210
And his channel, as many of you know,

00:21:09.210 --> 00:21:11.700
is focused on the gaming
and video game industry.

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:13.680
Things that Richard is
incredibly passionate about.

00:21:13.680 --> 00:21:15.450
This is exactly the kind of video

00:21:15.450 --> 00:21:19.830
that he would've loved to have
made if he'd been able to.

00:21:19.830 --> 00:21:21.960
His road to recovery is going to be long.

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:25.110
His sister has created a GoFundMe page

00:21:25.110 --> 00:21:27.090
called Reasonable Minds Can Rebuild,

00:21:27.090 --> 00:21:28.740
because one of Richard's famous phrases

00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:30.210
is that reasonable minds can differ,

00:21:30.210 --> 00:21:34.110
because he wasn't afraid to
offer an unpopular opinion

00:21:34.110 --> 00:21:36.360
because reasonable minds can differ

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:38.430
especially when it comes to the law.

00:21:38.430 --> 00:21:40.830
And that's always been an
incredibly admirable trait of his.

00:21:40.830 --> 00:21:42.450
So I put a link to his GoFundMe page

00:21:42.450 --> 00:21:44.460
down in the description and up on screen.

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:46.980
If you're able to support it,
it would mean the world to me

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:49.320
so that we can speed along his recovery

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:50.910
because legal commentary on YouTube

00:21:50.910 --> 00:21:53.760
is just a little bit dimmer
without his contributions.

00:21:53.760 --> 00:21:54.660
And then of course,

00:21:54.660 --> 00:21:57.180
he can deal with all these
frigging nerdy questions

00:21:57.180 --> 00:22:00.990
about "Dungeons &amp; Dragons"
and Wizards of the Coast.

00:22:00.990 --> 00:22:01.962
Bunch of nerds.

00:22:01.962 --> 00:22:02.850
(explosion booming)

00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:04.137
Cool kids play "Magic."

